      "question": "One of the recent oil samples from Transformer 1 at site MAIN shows a jump in hydrogen. Before anyone acts on it, I want to rule out a bad sample. A sample that picked up air during handling usually shows oxygen and nitrogen jumping at the same time.\n\nBetween 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000 and 2024-01-19T00:00:00.000000, screen hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen for anomalous samples with a proper anomaly detection model, not a fixed threshold. For each gas, list the flagged timestamps and their values. Mark a flagged hydrogen sample as 'suspect' if oxygen or nitrogen is also flagged at the same timestamp. Otherwise mark it as 'genuine'.\n\nThen build a second finding from the latest complete sample that isn't suspect. A complete sample has all of the following present:\n- hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene and acetylene\n- co, co2, oxygen and nitrogen\n- dbds, interfacial_v and dielectric_rigidity\n\nFrom that sample, give me:\n- a dissolved gas fault classification from the five fault gases\n- a health index and condition category from all twelve inputs\n\nIf the newest sample is itself suspect, say so, use the previous complete sample, and give me its timestamp.\n\nDecide as follows:\n- **Sampling artefact.** Every hydrogen flag is suspect, the class is not D1, D2 or T3, and the health index is fair or better. First check open work orders on Transformer 1 for an existing oil sampling job. If one exists, give me its number and don't duplicate it. Otherwise, create a priority 3 resample work order. In its notes, list the suspect timestamps and ask for a gas-tight syringe.\n- **Real fault.** At least one hydrogen flag is genuine and the class is PD, D1 or D2. Check open work orders the same way. If there's no match, create a priority 2 work order covering a confirmatory oil sample and a partial discharge investigation. Code it with the closest discharge failure code from our catalog.\n- **Disagreement.** The two findings disagree, for example a genuine hydrogen flag with a mild class, or only suspect flags with a D1, D2 or T3 class or a poor health index. Don't create a corrective job. Recommend a confirmatory resample within 7 days and tell me which finding disagreed.\n- **Nothing flagged.** If hydrogen isn't flagged at all, say the window looks stable and create nothing.\n\nIf any of hydrogen, oxygen or nitrogen has fewer than 4 non-null samples, skip the model for that gas. Instead, flag any sample more than 50% above that gas's window mean as a manual outlier, and say so. If an input for the health index is missing in every candidate sample, don't estimate it. Name the missing input and base the decision on the gas classification alone.",
      "answer": "I couldn't finish the screen, so I made no decision and created or changed no work orders. Your rules need a proper anomaly detection model and forbid a fixed threshold, and I couldn't run one on this window.\n\n**Why the screen is blocked**\n- **Data in the window:** Transformer 1 has 4 samples, on 01-15, 01-16, 01-17 and 01-18. Hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen each have 4 non-null values, so your \"fewer than 4\" manual fallback doesn't apply.\n| Timestamp | H\u2082 (ppm) | O\u2082 (ppm) | N\u2082 (ppm) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2024-01-15 | 2845 | 5860 | 27842 |\n| 2024-01-16 | 2950 | 5700 | 28000 |\n| 2024-01-17 | 2800 | 5900 | 27700 |\n| 2024-01-18 | 2900 | 5800 | 27950 |\n- **Detector:** the model catalog has no anomaly detection models. An inline detector (SubLOF) is accepted, but it needs a file as input. The time-series tools rejected a direct IoT data source, and I have no tool that can write the samples to a file.\n- **Earlier runs:** there are 11 anomaly runs on Transformer 1 in the run history. They don't record which gas or file they used, so I can't count them as your screen. One run flagged index 1, which would be the 01-16 sample if it used this window, but I can't tie it to a gas.\n\n**Second finding, provisional**\nThis uses the newest complete sample (2024-01-18), which is only valid if that sample turns out not to be suspect. All twelve inputs are present, and all four samples in the window are complete.\n- **Fault class: T2 (thermal fault, 300\u2013700 \u00b0C).** The fault classification tool returned \"LLM unavailable\", so I worked out the ratios by hand:\n  - C\u2082H\u2082/C\u2082H\u2084 = 0.0004, which is below 0.1\n  - CH\u2084/H\u2082 = 2.53, which is above 1\n  - C\u2082H\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086 = 3.05, which is between 1 and 4\n- **Health index: 71.9 (Good).**\n\n**Where this points**\n- If every hydrogen flag comes back suspect, T2 with a Good index is the sampling artefact case. If any flag is genuine, T2 isn't PD, D1 or D2, so it would be the disagreement case: the gas classification disagreed, and you'd get a resample within 7 days and no corrective job.\n- If the screen finds no hydrogen flags at all, the window counts as stable.\n- Looking at the numbers, hydrogen stays within about \u00b13% of its window mean. I'm giving that for context only, not as a flag.\n\n**Open work orders on Transformer 1**\n- **WO 1000052** (approved, priority 3): an oil resample (DGA) and insulation check for a T2 fault. If this turns out to be the sampling artefact case, this counts as the existing oil sampling job, so I wouldn't create a duplicate.\n- **WO 1000051** (awaiting approval, priority 1): an inspection of the on-load tap changer. It isn't an oil sampling job.\n\nTo finish, I need either a CSV of this window (timestamp, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen) at a file path, or an anomaly detector added to the catalog. With either one I can run the screen, mark each hydrogen flag suspect or genuine, and apply your rules.",
